WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:11.540
Origin and Referer validation can provide useful defense in depth for state-changing requests, especially when token validation is difficult or when additional assurance is desired.

2
00:00:11.640 --> 00:00:16.120
These headers can help the server reason about where a browser request appears to come from.

3
00:00:16.220 --> 00:00:25.420
They should be implemented carefully because proxies, privacy settings, deployment architecture, and browser behavior can affect what the application receives.

4
00:00:25.700 --> 00:00:29.300
CORS is often misunderstood in CSRF discussions.

5
00:00:29.400 --> 00:00:34.840
CORS controls which browser-based cross-origin responses are readable by frontend code.

6
00:00:34.940 --> 00:00:41.980
It is not, by itself, a complete CSRF protection model for cookie-authenticated state-changing requests.

7
00:00:42.080 --> 00:00:47.760
A request can be security-relevant even when the response is not readable by another origin.

8
00:00:48.040 --> 00:00:51.080
API boundaries deserve clear design.

9
00:00:51.180 --> 00:00:58.700
Browser clients using session cookies have different assumptions from non-browser clients using service credentials or bearer tokens.

10
00:00:58.800 --> 00:01:02.560
A Python backend that supports both should not mix the models casually.

11
00:01:02.660 --> 00:01:09.380
Each route should have an authentication method, CSRF expectation, and client assumption that reviewers can understand.

12
00:01:09.660 --> 00:01:13.820
The safest review posture is to match protections to the credential model.

13
00:01:13.920 --> 00:01:17.840
Session-authenticated browser flows usually need CSRF controls.

14
00:01:17.940 --> 00:01:24.180
Token-based APIs may have different protections, but they still need clear origin, authorization, and logging decisions.

15
00:01:24.280 --> 00:01:32.200
False confidence is the risk: CORS, Origin checks, cookies, and tokens each solve different parts of the problem.
